{"text": "87-295CL\nSeptember 9, 1987\n\nDr. Thomas volimuth\nLorillard Research\nP.O. Box 21688\n420 English Street\nGreensboro, NC 27420\n\nDear Dr. Vollmuth:\n\nIIT Research Institute (IITRI) is pleased to submit this proposal in\nresponse to your request for conduct of inhalation toxicology studies.\nDuring your recent visit to IITRI, and subsequent telephone\nconversations, several options regarding the type of studies to be\nconducted were discussed. We have incorporated your requirements into\nstudy design that will provide the necessary information, while\nkeeping the overall inhalation exposure period within the time frame\nrequested.\n\nThe overall objective of the proposed studies is to characterize the\ntoxic and teratologic potential of citral in a mammalian species\nexposed via inhalation. Prior to initiating the 21-day study,\ngeneration and monitoring techniques for the compound will be\ndeveloped. Thereafter, a five-day \"limit test\" will be conducted\nusing two exposure levels, one at the maximum attainable concentration\nin the vapor phase and one at 5 mg/L, or the maximum attainable\nconcentration in an aerosol/vapor mixture, as a range finding\ninvestigation to establish the effect level (if possible) for the\nlonger term 21-day study. Finally male and female rats will be\nexposed to vaporized and/or aerosolized citral on a 6-hr/day basis for\n21 consecutive days to establish mortality, clinical signs of\ntoxicity, body weight effects, clinical pathology changes, and gross\nand microscopic tissue alteration.\n\nTo examine the teratologic potential of citral, pregnant female rats\nwill be exposed to citral on days 6 through 15 of gestation at\nconcentrations similar to those of the 21-day inhalation toxicity\nstudy. This will be possible by coordinating the initjation of the\n21-day study with the mating of the females for the teratology\ninvestigation, so that exposures for the two separate studies may be\nconducted simultaneously in the same inhalation chambers. Mating of\nthe rats, observations, body weight measurement, and exposure will be\nconducted by IITRI personnel.\n\ne6ccotes",
